		     Interface Spec for SCHEME-81

	SCHEME-81 uses a two-phase non-overlapping clock, called PHI-1
and PHI-2.  All data is available to be latched by the falling edge of
PHI-1.  Control signals are generated during PHI-2 and must be stable
during the following PHI-1.  This convention is obeyed both internal
to the chip and in its transactions with the external world.


The SBUS

	  All devices which make up a SCHEME machine communicate
through a special bus called the SBUS.  Transactions on the SBUS are
in terms of SCHEME data objects which are either typed pointers or
typed immediate data (see SCHEME-81 architecture document).

All data transactions on the SBUS are formalized in terms of "external
registers", which are (possibly) ficticious registers.  Devices
communicate with each other by loading values into or reading values
from particular external reegisters.  Reading or loading an external
register may have control effects.  For example, loading a MAR
register initiates a memory cycle.

	External registers are accessed by control signals obeying the
same conventions as the control signals used to manipulate the
internal data paths of the SCHEME-81 chip.  The conventions are as
follows:

Registers latch their new values at the end of phi1

Registers can be read anytime, the old value is available during the
phi1 in which a new value is being latched;

Control signals for register operations are generated during phi2 and
are latched at the end of phi2;

Thus read signals can be directly applied to registers, load signals
must be ANDed with PHI-1.


	For example, when an external interrupt is granted by the
SCHEME-81 chip, the interrupt number (selecting the interrupt routine
to be run) is available in the INTERRUPT-NUMBER external register.
The SCHEME-81 chip accesses that value by asserting the read control
signal for the INTERRUPT-NUMBER register (I-NUM) during a PHI-2 and
then latching the value on the DATA[0-35] pads into one of its
internal registers during the next PHI-1.


Memory transactions

	The SCHEME-81 chip has a time multiplexed bus.  Memory
operations involve transmitting both the address to be accessed and
the data to be read or written on the same bus.  The bus can be
buffered and address latches may be used to demultiplex the bus as
desired.

	A memory transaction takes at least two clock cycles.  We
think of the clock cycles as beginning with PHI-2 (when control
signals are emitted) and ending with PHI-1 (when actions take effect
-- registers are loaded).  In the first cycle of a memory transaction,
the address to be accessed is loaded into an external register (either
READ-MAR or WRITE-MAR).  This initiates the memory transaction.  The
identity of the MAR register used determines whether the memory
transaction will be either a read or a write operation.  The memory
may be able to service the request immediately, or it may require
several clock cycles to respond.

	If the memory can service the request immediately, it must
inform the requester by pulling down the -MDONE line in time for the
requester to latch the decision in the PHI-2 following the address out
cycle.  The requester must either latch the data read in the next
PHI-1 or put out the data to be written so that it can be latched in
the next PHI-1.  The requester performs the data transfer by reading
from the READ-MBR external register or loading the WRITE-MBR external
register.  Following the conventions of register transfers, the data
to be read or written should begin to be supplied during the PHI-2
preceeding the PHI-1 in which it is to be latched.  The requester may
fail to complete the data transfer by not accessing the correct MBR.
The memory system will not modify an addressed location unless data is
written in the WRITE-MBR in the correct cycle.

	If the memory needs more time, it allows the -MDONE line to
remain high during each PHI-2 from the PHI-2 following the address out
cycle, until the PHI-2 immediately before the PHI-1 in which it
expects the data to be latched.  The -MDONE line must fall to be acted
upon in this PHI-2.

	If for some reason the memory fails to be able to accomplish
the requested transaction, it must assert S-ERROR in the same PHI-2
that -MDONE becomes asserted.  In this case the value on the SBUS is
invalid, and the SBUS-ERROR-CODE external register contains a
code describing the error.




PINOUT AND PAD CHARACTERISTICS
  
The Scheme-81 chip has the following pads:


TR-GND		top-right-ground
	ground pad
Ground for top row of pads.

PHI-2       top-PHI-2
	input pad

PHI-2 clock. 

C-GRANT		cons-server-interrupt-grant
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2. 

Indicates readiness of the storage allocator to produce a new cons
cell.  This happens as a result of some external device making a
cons-server request.  At the same time, the cons is being placed in
the SBUS-OUTPUT-CONS external register. (see pads: OCONS, -C-REQ,
DATA[0,35])

D-GRANT		dma-interrupt-grant
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2. 

Indicates that the SCHEME machine has given up control of the SBUS in
response to a dma-request. (see pads: -D-REQ)

E-GRANT        external-interrupt-grant.
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2. 

Indicates that the SCHEME machine is accepting a requested external interrupt.
The machine is also fetching the interrupt number from the INTERRUPT-NUMBER
external register. (see pads: -E-REQ, I-NUM, DATA[0,35])

HALTED		halted
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2. 

Indicates that the SCHEME machine has encountered a fatal error.
Something is broken.  This signal should never appear in a working machine. 

CARRY		carry-chain
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2. 

Indicates that the operation being performed in the current cycle uses
a carry chain.  This may be used to do clock-cycle hackery if carry
propagation limits the speed of the machine.

I-NUM           interrupt-number          
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for reading from the
INTERRUPT-NUMBER external register.  The device recieving E-GRANT
during this PHI-2 must drive the sbus with the interrupt number so
that it can be latched in the next PHI-1. (see pads: E-GRANT, -E-REQ,
DATA[0,35])

S-ECODE		sbus-error-code
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for reading from the
SBUS-ERROR-CODE external register.  The memory device handling the
proceeding memory operation must drive the sbus with the sbus-error
code (etc) (see pads: -READ-MAR, -READ-MBR, -WRITE-MAR, -WRITE-MBR, -MDONE,
-S-ERROR DATA[0,35]

OCONS		sbus-output-cons
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
SBUS-OUTPUT-CONS external register.  The device receiving C-GRANT
during this PHI-2 must latch the output cons in the next PHI-1.  (see
pads: C-GRANT, -C-REQ, DATA[0,35])

R-OVAL		sbus-output-relocated-value
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
SBUS-OUTPUT-RELOCATED-VALUE external register.  The external processor
requesting relocaton of a pointer during gc receives the result in
this register. The scheme machine will drive the sbus and the external
processor must latch the value in the next PHI-1. (see pads: RRTM,
R-IREQ, DATA[0,35])

R-IVAL		sbus-input-relocated-value
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for reading from the
SBUS-INPUT-RELOCATED-VALUE external register.  The external processor
supplying the relocaton of a pointer during gc must drive the sbus
with the result so that it can be latched during the next PHI-1. (see
pads: RRFMA, R-OREQ, DATA[0,35])

R-OREQ		sbus-output-relocate-request
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
SBUS-OUTPUT-RELOCATE-REQUEST external register to make a request for
pointer relocation.  The pointer to be relocated will be placed on the
sbus by the SCHEME machine and must be latched by the end of the next
PHI-1.  (see pads: R-IVAL, RRFMA, DATA[0,35])

R-IREQ		sbus-input-relocate-request
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for reading from the
SBUS-INPUT-RELOCATE-REQUEST external register.  The value in this
register is a pointer for this SCHEME machine to relocate.  The sbus
must be driven with the value so that it can be latched during the
next PHI-1. (see pads: RRTM, R-OVAL, DATA[0,35].)

-READ-MAR		read-mar
	pulldown pad, active low, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
READ-MAR external register.  This register contains the address to be
read from in memory.  Loading this register causes a memory operation
to begin. (see pads: -READ-MBR, -MDONE, -S-ERROR, S-ECODE, DATA[0,35], CDR)
					  
-WRITE-MAR		write-mar
	pulldown pad, active low, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
WRITE-MAR external register.  This register contains the address to be
written in memory.  Loading this register causes a memory operation to
begin. (see pads: -WRITE-MAR, -MDONE, -S-ERROR, S-ECODE, DATA[0,35], CDR)

-READ-MBR		read-mbr
	pulldown pad, active low, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for reading the
READ-MBR external register.  This register contains the data produced
by a memory operation.  This signal must be driven from the cycle
following a READ-MAR assertion, through the PHI-1 after the -MDONE
signal falls.  The value from memory must be driven onto the sbus so
that it can be latched during the next PHI-1 after -MDONE falls.  (see
pads: -READ-MAR, -MDONE, -S-ERROR, S-ECODE, DATA[0,35], CDR)

-WRITE-MBR		write-mbr
	pulldown-pad, active low, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This is the external register operation signal for loading the
WRITE-MBR external register.  This register contrins the data to be
stored into memory.  This signal must be driven from the cycle
following a WRITE-MAR assertion, through the PHI-1 after the -MDONE
signal falls.  The value must be driven onto the sbus during this same
period. (see pads: -WRITE-MAR, -MDONE, -S-ERROR, S-ECODE, DATA[0,35], CDR)

-ENABLE			-data-bus-enable
	output pad, active high, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This signal indicates that the SCHEME chip is transacting with the
data bus.

DATA-OUT		data-out
	output pad, active low, control signal output, latched by PHI-2.

This signal indicates the direction of a transaction with the
data-bus.  If the -ENABLE signal is asserted, and the DATA-OUT signal is
asserted, the SCHEME chip is writing on the SBUS; if the -ENABLE signal
is asserted and the DATA-OUT signal is not asserted, the SCHEME chip is
reading from the SBUS. If the -ENABLE signal is not asserted, the value
of DATA-OUT is irrevelant.

CDR			top-data-bus[0]
	tri-state, active high, address

This is the low bit of the data-bus. It is used to access the cdr of a pointer.
If this bit is on, the the scheme machine is referring to the cdr of a cell.  

DATA[0,7]               top-data-bus[1,8]
	tri-state, active high, address/data bus
					  
The bottom 8 bits of data.


TL-GND			top-left-ground
	One of two grounds for the internals of the chip.


VDDTOP			top-power
	One of two sources of VDD.

DATA[8,29]		side-data-bus
	tri-state, active high, address/data bus

The middle 22 bits of data

BL-GND			bottom-left-ground
	Ground for the side and bottom pads

VDDBOT			bottom-vdd
	One of two sources of VDD.


DATA[30,35]		bottom-data-bus
	tri-state, active high, address/data bus

The top 6 bits of data.

-MDONE			-memory-done
	buffered input pad, active low

This signal indicates that a memory operation is about to complete.
The signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  If it is
low, the memory asserts that it can drive or accept data on the next
phi1. (see pads: -READ-MAR, -READ-MBR, WRITE-MAR, WRITE-MBR, -SERROR)

FREEZE			freeze
	buffered input pad, active high

This signal freezes the scheme machine.  This signal must be stable in
time to be latched by phi2.  It prevents all registers from loading,
and causes the machine to cycle in the state it was in when the signal
became asserted. (see pad: LD-ST)

RRFMA			relocate-request-from-me-acknowledged
	buffered input pad, active high (?)

This signal indicates that the answer to a relocation request from the
current scheme machine to another processor has been answered.  The
signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  If active, it
indicates that the relocated value is available in the
SBUS-INPUT-RELOCATED-VALUE external register and can be fetched as
soon as the next phi1. (see pads: R-IVAL, R-OREQ) 

RRTM			relocate-request-to-me
	buffered input pad, active high (?)

The signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  If asserted,
it indicates that a relocation request has been made to the current
scheme machine, the pointer to be relocated is in the
SBUS-INPUT-RELOCATE-REQUEST external register and can be fetched as
soon as the next phi1. (see pads: R-OVAL, R-IREQ)

-NEED			-somebody-needs-gc
	pulldown/input pad, active low

Indicates that a processor is requesting the conspiracy to begin a
garbage collection.  This signal remains asserted during the entire gc
cycle and each processor involved must hold it dowm until it is
finished. (see pads: 

-READY			-somebody-isnt-ready-to-gc
	pulldown/input pad, active low

Indicates that a processor is unable to begin garbage collection.
This signal will go high when all processors are ready to garbage
collect. It is used to synchronize the beginning of garbage
collection. 

LD-ST			load-state
	buffered input pad, active high

Controls the direction of the STATE pads.  If high, the state pads
may be driven to set the state of the machine.  This should only be
attempted when FREEZE is high.  If this signal is low (default) the
state pads continuously output the current state of the control pla.

-E-REQ			external-interrupt-request
	input pad, active low

Indicates that another device is requesting an external interrupt.
The signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  The device
requesting the interrupt must be prepared to deliver an interrupt
number when the interupt is granted.

-D-REQ			dma-interrupt-request
	input pad, active low

Indicates that another device is requesting a dma interrupt.  The
signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  When the grant
is received the requesting device has complete control of the sbus and
retains such control until he releases the request.

-C-REQ			cons-server-interrupt-request
	input pad, active low

Indicates that another device is requesting a cons to be allocated.
The signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  The device
must be prepared to receive the cons.  The new cons will be gc-unsafe
and thus the requesting device must place legal objects in the car and
cdr of the new cons before gc occurs.  One way to do this is to
request DMA during the cycle the cons is being served. 

-S-ERROR		sbus-error-flag
	buffered input pad, active low

Indicates that an error has occured during a sbus operation.  The
signal must be stable in time to be latched by phi2.  The memory must
be prepared to place an error code in the SBUS-ERROR-CODE external
register.

STATE[0,8]		state
	tri-state, data-bus

These pads are used to set and monitor the state of the control PLA.
Their direction is controled by the LD-ST pad.

PHI-1			PHI-1
	input pad

PHI-1 clock.

BR-GND			bottom-right-ground
	ground pad
one of two grounds for the internals of the machine.

